      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find \\(\\sin A\\) in the right triangle \\(ABC\\) with \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), we need to identify the lengths of the sides involved. Since \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), triangle \\(ABC\\) is a right triangle with \\(AB\\) and \\(BC\\) as the legs and \\(AC\\) as the hypotenuse.\n\nWe are given:\n- \\(AB = 16\\)\n- \\(BC = 24\\)\n\nFirst, we need to find the length of the hypotenuse \\(AC\\). According to the Pythagorean theorem, for a right triangle with legs \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) and hypotenuse \\(c\\), we have:\n\\[\nc^2 = a^2 + b^2\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given lengths of \\(AB\\) and \\(BC\\):\n\\[\nAC^2 = AB^2 + BC^2\n\\]\n\n\\[\nAC^2 = 16^2 + 24^2\n\\]\n\n\\[\nAC^2 = 256 + 576\n\\]\n\n\\[\nAC^2 = 832\n\\]\n\n\\[\nAC = \\sqrt{832} = \\sqrt{16 \\times 52} = 4\\sqrt{52} = 4 \\times 2\\sqrt{13} = 8\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\n\nNow, we have:\n- \\(AB = 16\\)\n- \\(BC = 24\\)\n- \\(AC = 8\\sqrt{13}\\)\n\nThe sine of \\(\\angle A\\) in a right triangle is defined as the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the hypotenuse. Here, the side opposite to \\(\\angle A\\) is \\(BC\\) and the hypotenuse is \\(AC\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\sin A = \\frac{BC}{AC} = \\frac{24}{8\\sqrt{13}}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify this fraction, we can rationalize the denominator by multiplying both the numerator and the denominator by \\(\\sqrt{13}\\):\n\\[\n\\sin A = \\frac{24 \\times \\sqrt{13}}{8\\sqrt{13} \\times \\sqrt{13}} = \\frac{24\\sqrt{13}}{8 \\times 13} = \\frac{24\\sqrt{13}}{104} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\boxed{\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}}",
